/*!
    \page vscodeext-how-to-install.html
    \previouspage vscodeext-getting-started.html
    \nextpage vscodeext-how-tos.html

    \title Installation

    Install \QVSC to develop Qt and Qt Quick applications with VS Code.
    Select the Qt extension pack for the type of applications you develop:

    \list
        \li Qt C++ for developing Qt C++ and Qt Quick applications
        \li Qt Python for developing applications with Qt for Python
        \li Qt WebAssembly for developing applications for WebAssembly
    \endlist

    You can also install individual extensions with or without extension packs.
    \e {Qt Extension Pack} contains all Qt extensions. The extensions are
    activated when you need them in a project.

    \section1 Install extension packs

    To install a Qt extension pack:

    \list 1
        \li Select the \uicontrol {Extensions} icon in the
            \uicontrol {Activity Bar} or press \key {Ctrl+Shift+X}.
        \li In \uicontrol {Extensions}, search for Qt extension packs.
            \image vscodeext-installation.webp {Qt extensions in VS Code}
        \li Select \uicontrol {Install}.
    \endlist

    The \l {CMake For VisualStudio Code} and \l {CMake Tools} extensions are
    installed automatically.

    \section1 View extension status

    You can turn on automatic updates for \QVSC and switch between the latest
    release and a pre-release. However, you should keep the releases in sync.
    Specifically, you must use the same release of the Qt C++ and Qt Core
    extension.

    To see the version and activation status of each installed extension, select
    the version list in \uicontrol {Welcome}.

    \image {vscodeext-extension-status.webp} {Details of installed extensions.}

    Select \uicontrol {Refresh} to update the information about extension status.

    \sa {Register Qt installations}, {Set up Python development environment}
*/

/*!
    \page vscodeext-how-to-set-up-pyside6.html
    \previouspage vscodeext-getting-started.html
    \nextpage vscodeext-how-tos.html

    \title Set up Python development environment

    \include {vscodeext-overview.qdoc} {python-extension-overview}

    \section1 Install PySide6 for the project

    The recommended way to run PySide6 projects from VS Code is to install
    PySide6 for each project. \QVSC prompts you to do so the first time you open
    a \c {.py} file for a project.

    To install PySide6 for the project later:

    \list 1
        \li Go to \uicontrol {Command Palette}, and select
            \uicontrol {Qt: Install PySide6}.
        \li Select \uicontrol {Create environment} to create a new virtual
            environment for the project. To use an existing environment, select
            \uicontrol {Select environment}.
    \endlist

    \section1 Create virtual environment with uv

    If you have installed the \l {uv} Python package manager and set it in PATH,
    you can use it to create the virtual environment:

    \list 1
        \li Go to \uicontrol {Command Palette}, and select
            \uicontrol {Qt: Create virtual environment with uv}.
        \li Select \uicontrol {Create environment} to create a new virtual
            environment for the project. To use an existing environment, select
            \uicontrol {Select environment}.
    \endlist

    \sa {Installation}, {Tutorial: Qt Quick and Qt for Python},
    {Create Qt projects}, {Debug Qt for Python applications},
    {Read Qt documentation}
*/
